#pragma once

// Förklaringen som följer med en sparad analys, skriven för en AI som inte vet något om Kickbase: vad pluginet är, vad datan är, hur tiden
// hänger ihop med mönstret (pianorullen) och effekterna, och hur man letar efter fel och lösningar. JSON, i flera delar (en strängkonstant får
// inte vara för lång i MSVC).
namespace AnalysisAbout
{
    inline const char* const parts[] = {
R"KB({
"what_this_file_is": "A complete audio analysis of one loop of a psytrance project made in the synthesizer plugin Kickbase. The plugin played its own pattern through once, offline, and measured the kick, every instrument and the master output, bar by bar and frame by frame. The file contains what was measured (spectra, harmonics, kick hits, ducking), a list of findings the plugin's own analyzer made (collisions, masking, tuning, rumble, limiter) with the bars and frequencies, and ALL the synth's settings and the notes of the pattern, so that you can connect what you see in the measurements to what causes it, and suggest or work out fixes. You (an AI or a person) are expected to read the measurements together with the settings and the notes, find concrete problems, and say exactly which parameter to change and to what.",
"plugin": {
  "name": "Kickbase (also Kickbase FX, a sibling plugin that gates and processes another synth)",
  "type": "VST3 instrument plugin for Windows, made for psytrance (fast, rolling basslines of 140-150 BPM with a kick every beat, built so that kick and bass work together)",
  "idea": "One kick drum synth plus up to 10 instrument synths (usually basses and leads, named Instr 1-10, internally 'bass1'-'bass10'). A pattern sequencer plays them in sync with the DAW. The note the DAW sends (for example E) sets the key: the kick's tail and the instruments' root follow it. The kick is made to sit in the key and the basses are made to leave room for it (sidechain ducking).",
  "kick": "A kick synth: body oscillator with a pitch envelope that sweeps down to the 'tail' frequency (the pitched boom, normally 40-55 Hz in psytrance), amplitude envelope, harmonics, a click/transient layer, noise, drive, saturation, low cut/high cut, and an optional sample. Parameters start with 'kick'.",
  "instruments": "Each instrument has three wavetable oscillators (OSC 1-3 in the plugin, A, B, C in the parameter ids; each with a wavetable choice out of ~120 tables, a position in the table, level, semitones, fine tune, unison voices with detune and stereo spread, warp modes such as sync/bend/pwm/mirror/fm/flip/quantize/am, phase), a sub oscillator, noise, a resonant filter (low pass 12/24 dB, high pass, band pass) with its own envelope and key tracking, an amp ADSR, glide notes (a note marked as glide bends the sounding note to its pitch), drive, an optional sample, two more modulation envelopes (ENV 2/3), a pitch envelope and a gate (an on/off chopping rhythm with its own grid). Parameters start with 'bass1' ... 'bass10' (bass1 = Instr 1).",
  "pattern": "Notes are placed on a grid (ticks). The kick and each instrument have their own row with its own length in bars; a row shorter than the loop repeats if its LOOP switch is on. Rows can be step patterns or a piano roll. The notes are listed in synth.pattern, and in 'pattern_timeline' converted to bars and beats.",
  "effects": "11 effect channels, FX1-FX11 (ids use letters: FX1 = 'fxA', FX2 = 'fxB' ... FX11 = 'fxK'), each with 5 effect slots in series, then an EQ, a compressor, a level and a duck amount. Effect types: Filter, Distortion, EQ, Chorus, Phaser, Echo, Reverb, Compressor, Trance Gate, Comb, Ring Mod, Bitcrusher, Auto Filter, Maximizer, and Sidechain (ducks the channel by the level of the kick or of one instrument). Each instrument and the kick can send an amount of its sound to one effect channel (parameters bassNFxChannel and bassNFxAmount, kickFxChannel/kickFxAmount; channel 0 = none). What is sent goes through the channel's effects; the rest stays dry. NOTE: the measured instrument signals in this file are taken BEFORE the effect sends, so effect tails (echo, reverb) are only in the Master signal.",
  "sidechain": "The kick ducks the instruments: each instrument has a DUCK amount (bassNDuck, 0-1) and the shape of the ducking is the kick's own envelope or a drawn curve (synth.sidechain_curve). This is what makes the bass get out of the way of the kick. The measurements here are taken after ducking.",
  "master": "Master section after the mixer: width, mono-below-frequency, stereo enhance, drive, clip, and a limiter with a ceiling (parameters starting 'master'). The limiter's gain reduction is reported as limiter_max_reduction_db.",
  "eq_per_source": "The kick, every instrument and the master each have their own EQ (high pass, four bands: low shelf, two bells, high shelf, and low pass). Parameter ids: kickEq1F/kickEq1G/kickEq1Q ... kickEqHp, kickEqLp; bassNEq1F ... bassNEqHp; masterEq... (F = frequency Hz, G = gain dB, Q). Neutral by default.",
  "modulation": "Three LFOs, four macros and per-instrument envelopes/velocity/random can modulate almost any sound parameter through a matrix (synth.lfo_routes). Modulation changes the sound over time, so the measured spectrum can move within a loop for that reason.",
  "ui": "The window has a top bar (logo, AI link, preset, export, previews, tempo/bar/key), an upper panel with the tabs PATTERN, KICK, INSTRUMENT, AI, and a lower panel with the tabs EFFECTS, SIDECHAIN, LFO, ENV, MATRIX, MASTER, SOUNDSTAGE, ANALYZER, SETTINGS. The analysis in this file comes from the ANALYZER tab (views SPECTRUM and ANALYSIS + REPORT)."
},
)KB",
R"KB("signal_flow": [
  "Each instrument: oscillators -> filter -> drive -> amp envelope * velocity * level -> ducking by the kick (DUCK) -> the instrument's EQ -> [measured here: 'Instr N'] -> split: the amount sent to its FX channel goes through the effects, the rest is dry -> mixer (gain, pan, ducking choice) -> master.",
  "Kick: kick synth -> the kick's EQ -> [measured here: 'Kick'] -> optional FX send -> mixer -> master.",
  "Master: sum of the mixer -> master EQ -> width/mono/enhance -> drive -> clip -> limiter -> output [measured here: 'Master', after the limiter].",
  "The measured source signals are mono sums (left+right)/2."
],
"analysis_method": {
  "how": "The plugin makes a private copy of itself with the same settings and pattern, and renders the pattern offline at 48 kHz at the project's tempo (the key note is the one the DAW last sent). It first plays a pre-roll of one or more whole pattern lengths so that effect tails, LFOs and notes that run across the loop boundary are in the right state, then measures exactly one pass of the loop (at most 16 bars). Every 43-53 ms it takes a Hann-windowed FFT of 8192 samples (a 171 ms window, about 5.9 Hz per FFT bin) of each source's last 8192 samples.",
  "bands": "The FFT power is summed into frequency bands (meta.band_edges_hz): fine in the bass (about 10 Hz wide under 100 Hz, then 20-80 Hz wide up to 500 Hz) and roughly critical-band wide above. Band level (dB) = 10*log10(summed power / 1.5), normalised so that a full-scale sine wave reads about 0 dB; levels below -90 are essentially silence.",
  "findings": "Findings come from the plugin's analyzer. A source counts as 'playing' in a band only if its level is above a floor (45 dB, 35 or 55 dB for low/high sensitivity) below the loudest band of the mix AND within 24 dB of its own loudest band (so the skirts of a spectrum do not count). Two sources 'collide' in a band when both play and are within 6 dB of each other, and only if this holds in enough of the frames of a bar (50%, at least 3 frames; adjustable with sensitivity). 'Masking' means an instrument is more than 6 dB louder than the kick in the kick's main bands while the kick plays. Kick/instrument overlap in the bass is capped at 'warning' because it is normal when the sidechain gives the kick the hit and the instrument the gaps; octaves and unison between two pitched sources count as in tune, a distance of about one semitone from the same note is flagged as beating/out of tune. Measurements are the ground truth; the findings are a first interpretation and you may disagree with them.",
  "fundamentals": "The fundamental of a source is the lowest clear spectral peak between 28 and 400 Hz that is within 10 dB of the strongest peak there (a bass often has a stronger second harmonic than fundamental). The kick's tail frequency (kick_tail_hz) is measured in the time domain: the period of the kick signal 120-340 ms after each hit, median over the hits (kick_tail_measured_in says which method was used; the average spectrum is only a fallback). Spectral fundamentals below about 40 Hz have limited resolution (5.9 Hz per bin), so treat them as +-3 Hz.",
  "ducking": "For every kick hit the lowest ducking gain of each instrument within 100 ms after the hit is reported in dB (0 = not ducked, 12 = ducked 12 dB)."
},
)KB",
R"KB("data_layout": {
  "meta": "Units, frame timing, bpm, bars analysed, sample rate, the list of band edges and the names of the sources. Index into the band arrays with meta.band_edges_hz: band i covers band_edges_hz[i] to band_edges_hz[i+1].",
  "findings": "The plugin analyzer's findings: severity (problem/warning/info), category (collision, masking, kick, sub, master, reference), title, detail (an explanation with a suggested fix), sources, low_hz/high_hz, bars (1-based bar numbers in the loop where it happens) and share_of_bars. Number n in the list = finding n.",
  "ai_notes": "Notes an AI has already written into the plugin's report: title, text, finding (1-based number it refers to, 0 = general) and applied (true = the change was made).",
  "kick_tail_hz / kick_tail_design_hz / limiter_max_reduction_db": "The kick's tail frequency as MEASURED (kick_tail_measured_in says how: the period of the kick signal 120-340 ms after each hit, median over the hits), the tail frequency the kick SHOULD have according to its settings (key + TUNE, kick_tail_design_hz) and the most the master limiter reduced the level in the loop (dB). If they differ by less than ~1 semitone the kick is where it was designed to be. Band levels (band_db, peak_band_db) are levels of the energy inside one frequency band, not sample peaks: a value above 0 dB there is NOT clipping (use sources[].sample_peak_db / rms_db (whole loop) and frames.peak_db for the real sample peaks).",
  "sources": "One entry per source (Kick, Instr 1..N as the user named them, Master): peak_band_db, fundamental_hz and note, average_band_db[band] (power average over the whole loop) and max_band_db[band].",
  "per_bar": "For every source, an array 'bars' with one entry per bar: bar (1-based), frames, loudness_db, average_band_db[band] and max_band_db[band] within the bar, fundamental_hz and note for that bar, harmonics (the harmonic series: n, hz, db, db_vs_fundamental = how many dB above or below the fundamental; harmonic n is looked for within 2% of n times the fundamental), and spectrum_12th_octave_db (120 values in 1/12-octave steps starting at 20 Hz; the centre frequencies are in spectrum_12th_octave_centre_hz).",
  "kick_hits": "Every kick hit: bar, beat (1.0 = first beat of the bar, 4.99 = end of the bar), time_s from the start of the loop, and ducking_db for each instrument.",
  "frames": "(only in the saved file) The spectrogram: the level of every band in every frame through the whole loop: seconds[frame], bar[frame] (the bar the frame belongs to) and band_db[source][frame][band]; plus beat_in_bar[frame] (1.0-4.99, to join with pattern_timeline), rms_db, peak_db and centroid_hz per source and frame, ducking_db per instrument and frame (how deep the sidechain ducks it, 0 = not ducked), limiter_reduction_db[frame], and how the modulation moves through the loop: modulation_sources (every LFO, macro, envelope, velocity or random source that is routed somewhere, its value in each frame) and modulated_parameters (the real value in each frame of every parameter that has a modulation route, in the own unit of the parameter, so you can see for example how a filter cutoff or an effect knob moves). Use it to see how the spectrum and level move inside a bar, for example how fast a bass recovers after a kick, or when a bass note starts relative to the kick.",
  "synth": "All the synth's settings when the analysis started: parameters (every parameter by id with its value in real units, wavetables as names, switches as true/false), effects (the used effect slots with their knobs), the pattern (every row's notes as [tick, length, pitch, velocity, glide], its grid, whether it is a piano roll, the gate), kick envelopes, the sidechain curve and the LFO routes.",
  "dynamics": "(in sources and per_bar) rms_db and peak_db: the source's average and highest level; crest_db = peak - rms (low = squashed/dense, high = punchy transients); centroid_hz: the brightness (power-weighted mean frequency; a bass is ~60-200 Hz, a kick body lower, higher = brighter or noisier); energy_by_range_db: the energy in six ranges (sub_20_60, bass_60_250, low_mid_250_500, mid_500_2k, high_mid_2k_6k, high_6k_20k) so you can see at a glance where a source's energy is and compare sources and bars.",
  "presets": "Which presets are loaded: Full, Kick, Pattern, Sidechain, Master (the maximizer), Soundstage, LFO, Effects (one FX channel) and one per instrument (empty = none loaded or Default). A preset is only the starting point: the settings below are what actually plays.",
  "synth_overview": "ALL settings arranged by what they belong to, so you do not have to decode parameter ids: kick (+ kick_eq), instruments[] (each with routing_and_levels incl. DUCK and the FX send channel/amount, pitch, oscillators A/B/C with the wavetable NAME, position, level, semitones, fine, unison voices/detune/blend/stereo, warp mode and amount, phase; sub_and_noise, filter, amp_envelope, drive, gate_chopper, sample, modulation envelopes 2 and 3, pitch_envelope, eq), effect_channels[] (FX1-FX11 that are used: level_and_duck, slots_in_order with effect type and knobs, eq, compressor), master (+ master_eq), mixer, sidechain, modulation_sources (LFOs, macros), modulation_routes, pattern_settings (loop length, row lengths and loop switches), kick_envelopes, sidechain_curve, lfo_custom_curves, samples_loaded and user_wavetables_loaded (names only, the audio data itself is not in the file). 'parameter_prefix' says which parameter ids to use when proposing a change (for example bass2 + Cutoff = bass2Cutoff).",
  "filters": "meta.filters_applied shows if the data was limited to some sources, bars, a frequency range (then every band array only holds meta.bands_included.first_band_index..last_band_index) or every n-th frame. An empty filter means everything.",
  "pattern_timeline": "The pattern converted to the loop's own time: for every bar of the loop and every instrument the notes that start in that bar with beat position, length in beats, pitch (semitones from the key root) and velocity. Rows shorter than the loop are repeated if their loop switch is on. Use it to see which notes play in the bars where the analysis found a problem."
},
)KB",
R"KB("time_axis": {
  "bars": "Bar numbers start at 1 at the start of the analysed loop (the first bar of the pattern) and are the same in the findings, per_bar, kick_hits, frames and pattern_timeline. 4/4 time: 4 beats per bar; at the file's bpm one beat is 60/bpm seconds.",
  "beats": "beat 1.0 is the start of the bar; a 1/16 step is 0.25 beat. In the pattern, one bar = synth.pattern.ticks_per_bar ticks, so beat = 1 + (tick mod ticks_per_bar) / (ticks_per_bar / 4) and bar = floor(tick / ticks_per_bar) + 1.",
  "seconds": "time_s and frames.seconds are seconds from the start of the loop. A frame's time is the middle of its 171 ms window, so an event shows up in frames about 85 ms before and 85 ms after it.",
  "comparing": "To find the cause of something at bar B beat X: look up the notes in pattern_timeline[B] near that beat (which instruments play, which pitch), the kick_hits of that bar (is the kick there, how deep are the instruments ducked), and the effect slots and sends in synth (is something sent to an echo or reverb that rings over the hit, is there a Trance Gate or Sidechain effect), then the settings of the instruments involved (octave, tune, filter cutoff, sub, DUCK, EQ)."
},
)KB",
R"KB("how_to_analyse": [
  "1. Read meta and the list of findings first. Sort them by severity and by how many bars they cover (share_of_bars). Check ai_notes to see what has already been suggested or fixed.",
  "2. For each finding, open per_bar for the sources involved at the bars named, compare average_band_db in the finding's frequency range (low_hz-high_hz): who is louder, by how many dB, is it the same in every bar or only some. A difference under 6 dB means they compete; over 6-10 dB the louder one hides the other.",
  "3. Check the fundamentals (per source and bar) against the kick's tail (kick_tail_hz) and each other: the same note or an octave is fine, about one semitone apart (ratio 1.059, e.g. 41 Hz and 43.5 Hz) beats and sounds out of tune, a fifth (ratio 1.5) or a fourth (1.33) is consonant. Use harmonics to see whose overtones fall on top of another's fundamental.",
  "4. For anything involving the kick: look at kick_hits[*].ducking_db. Under 3 dB means the instrument is barely ducked; over 9 dB means it is ducked a lot, so remaining overlap is its tail after the kick. Then look at frames.band_db for the instrument in the 30-150 Hz bands just after each kick (seconds in kick_hits) to see how fast it recovers.",
  "5. Compare with the pattern: pattern_timeline shows which notes start in the problem bars. A long sustained note over many kicks collides every time; a note that starts exactly on the kick collides at the hit; two instruments playing the same pitch in the same register collide everywhere they overlap.",
  "6. Look at the master: limiter_max_reduction_db (over 5 dB means everything together is too loud for the ceiling and the limiter squashes the kick), the master's average_band_db balance (mud around 150-400 Hz, harshness 2-5 kHz, rumble under 30 Hz) and compare with a reference if the file has findings with category 'reference'.",
  "7. Propose changes as exact parameter ids and values (see 'fixes'), in order of effect and risk, and say which measurement you expect to improve. After a change the analysis should be run again and compared."
],
"what_to_look_for": [
  "Collisions: two sources within 6 dB of each other in the same band at the same time in many frames of a bar. Worst in 40-250 Hz (the bass and the kick) and in 200-500 Hz (mud).",
  "The kick covered by an instrument: an instrument 6+ dB louder than the kick in the kick's main bands (about 40-120 Hz body, 2-5 kHz click) at the kick hits. Typical cause: too little DUCK, a long release, an instrument note starting on the kick, or the instrument too loud.",
  "Kick tail out of range: tail below about 30 Hz (inaudible on many speakers, wastes headroom) or above about 62 Hz (thin, conflicts with the bass); it should normally be 40-55 Hz and in tune with the bass root (same note or octave).",
  "Out-of-tune instruments: fundamentals that are about 0.7-1.4 semitones from the same note as the kick tail or another instrument.",
  "Rumble: strong energy under 30 Hz in an instrument or the master.",
  "Limiter working hard: reduction of 5 dB or more, or peaks that are always at the ceiling.",
  "Instruments with no ducking that overlap the kick in the bass; instruments ducked so hard they vanish.",
  "A spectrum that is too empty or too full: bands with no energy where the style needs it (a missing midrange between the bass and the highs, no click on the kick) or all instruments in the same 3-4 bands.",
  "Things that change inside a bar: LFO or envelope movement, notes that change pitch, effects that swell, visible in frames and per-bar differences."
],
"fixes": [
  "Duck more under the kick: raise bassNDuck (0-1) for that instrument; ducking depth is also shaped in the plugin's SIDECHAIN tab (the curve) and the sidechain release.",
  "Duck one instrument under another: add an effect of type Sidechain in a free FX channel (knobs SOURCE = Kick or Instr 1-10, THRESHOLD dB, DEPTH 0-1, ATTACK ms, RELEASE ms) and send the instrument that should duck 100% to that channel (bassNFxChannel = that channel number, bassNFxAmount = 1).",
  "Clear the low end for the kick: high-pass the higher instrument (bassNEqHp 100-200 Hz) so only one instrument owns the 40-100 Hz range, or cut a bell (bassNEq2F near the kick tail, bassNEq2G -3 to -6 dB, Q 1-2).",
  "Give instruments different registers: change bassNOctave or bassNTune so fundamentals do not coincide, but stay on the same note or an octave/fifth of the kick tail.",
  "Retune: kickTune (semitones) moves the kick tail; bassNTune (semitones) and bassNOctave move an instrument; aim for the kick tail at 40-55 Hz and on a note of the key.",
  "Remove rumble: bassNEqHp or masterEqHp 30-40 Hz, or raise the instrument's octave.",
  "Lower or raise levels: bassNLevel (instrument; scales its dry and its FX-sent part), kickLevel, mixer gains (mixBassNGain, mixKickGain scale only the dry part; the part sent to an FX channel is scaled by that channel's strip mixFxAGain...mixFxKGain); lowering the loudest instruments is better than letting the limiter work (masterCeiling, masterDrive).",
  "Shorten sustains: bassNRelease, bassNDecay or the note length in the pattern, so a note does not ring over the next kick; or move the note start away from the kick.",
  "Tame mud: a cut around 200-400 Hz (bassNEq3F/G) on the instruments; harshness: a cut or low pass in the 2-5 kHz range.",
  "Effects that smear the kick: check which effect channels get sent to; reduce bassNFxAmount for long echoes/reverbs on bass, add a Sidechain effect or raise the channel's own duck (fxXDuck).",
  "Always tell the user what you changed and why, change as little as possible, and re-run the analysis to confirm."
],
"limitations": [
  "The measurement is mono; stereo width and phase problems are not measured (only reported through the master's width setting).",
  "Instrument signals are measured before the effect sends, so effect tails are only visible in Master.",
  "Resolution: 5.9 Hz per bin and 171 ms per frame; very fast transients (a kick click shorter than ~20 ms) are smeared in time, and frequencies under 40 Hz are approximate.",
  "Only one pass of the loop is analysed (the pattern's own length, at most 16 bars). If the DAW arrangement changes things over time (automation, other tracks), that is not included.",
  "The analysis cannot hear: levels are physical measurements. Taste (a gritty bass, a distorted kick) is not a problem just because it creates harmonics. Treat 'info' findings as hints only."
]
})KB"
    };
}
